  "body": {
    "id": "prop:one-path-limit",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "For every fixed \\(t_0\\in(0,1/4)\\) in the witness submodel with \\(\\pi_0=\\pi_1=1/2\\), the unique surviving path satisfies \\(d_H(S_s,S_t)=2|s-t|\\) locally.  With the clipped estimator \\(\\widehat t=\\min\\{1/4,\\max\\{0,\\{\\widehat q_1(1,3)-\\widehat q_1(1,1)\\}/2\\}\\}\\),\n\\[\n\\sqrt n\\,d_H(S_{\\widehat t},S_{t_0})\\Rightarrow2\\sqrt{1/4-2t_0^2}\\,|N(0,1)|.\n\\]\nPlug-in folded-normal quantiles give pointwise first-order Hausdorff coverage.",
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:positive-instrument-shares",
      "ass:iid-sampling",
      "def:discontinuity-witness",
      "thm:continuity-frontier"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "For \\(s,t>0\\) sufficiently close to the fixed interior value \\(t_0\\), the common dual certificate in the metric-impossibility proof excludes the same five paths, and the surviving path gives\n\\[\nS_t=\\{(7/8,r,9/8+2t):1\\le r\\le1+2t\\}.\n\\]\nIf \\(s\\ge t\\), every point of \\(S_t\\) is matched by clamping its middle coordinate into \\([1,1+2s]\\), while the third coordinate moves by \\(2(s-t)\\); the upper endpoint of \\(S_s\\) attains that discrepancy.  Reversing \\(s,t\\) proves\n\\[\nd_H(S_s,S_t)=2|s-t|.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nWithin arm \\(Z=1\\), define \\(X=1/2\\) on the treated cell with outcome \\(2\\), \\(X=-1/2\\) on the treated cell with outcome \\(0\\), and \\(X=0\\) otherwise.  Under \\(q_{t_0}\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb EX=t_0,\\qquad\n\\mathbb EX^2=\\frac14\\{(1/4+t_0)+(1/4-t_0)\\}=\\frac18,\n\\]\nso \\(\\operatorname{Var}(X)=1/8-t_0^2\\).  The characteristic-function calculation for bounded iid variables, followed by the random-arm ratio expansion, gives\n\\[\n\\sqrt n(\\widehat t-t_0)\\Rightarrow\nN\\!\\left(0,\\frac{1/8-t_0^2}{\\pi_1}\\right)\n=N(0,1/4-2t_0^2),\n\\]\nwhere the last equality uses \\(\\pi_1=1/2\\).  Because \\(t_0\\) is interior, clipping changes the raw contrast with probability tending to zero.  Combining this limit with (1) and continuity of absolute value yields the displayed folded-normal law.  Replacing \\(t_0\\) by \\(\\widehat t\\) in the continuous positive variance \\(1/4-2t_0^2\\) is consistent, so its folded-normal upper quantile yields pointwise first-order coverage.",
    "justification": "The solved one-path stratum verifies that the regular branch is nonvacuous and provides a calibration check and special case of the general active-germ recovery theorem.",
    "gap": "Generic nonsmooth-bound asymptotics do not state this explicit rank-policy folded-normal loss law.",
    "consumer": "The proposition supplies a calibration benchmark and a mandatory regression test for the full loss compiler.",
